Certainly! Setting up SSL termination with Nginx is a common practice to ensure secure communication between clients and your server. Here's a step-by-step guide to configure Nginx to terminate SSL and forward plain HTTP traffic to your backend services.

### Step 1: Obtain SSL Certificates

You can obtain SSL certificates from a trusted Certificate Authority (CA) or use a free service like Let's Encrypt. For this example, we'll assume you have your SSL certificate (`fullchain.pem`) and private key (`privkey.pem`).

### Step 2: Install Nginx

If you haven't already installed Nginx, you can do so using the following commands:

```sh
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install nginx
```

### Step 3: Configure Nginx for SSL Termination

Create a new configuration file in `/etc/nginx/sites-available/` and enable it by creating a symbolic link in `/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/`.

#### 1. Create the Configuration File

Create a file named `ssl_termination` in `/etc/nginx/sites-available/`:

```sh
sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/ssl_termination
```

Add the following configuration to the file:

```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name your_domain.com;

    ssl_certificate /path/to/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /path/to/privkey.pem;

    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers HIGH:!aNULL:!MD5;
    ssl_prefer_server_ciphers on;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://backend_servers;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}

upstream backend_servers {
    server backend_server1:8080;
    server backend_server2:8080;
    server backend_server3:8080;
}
```

Replace `your_domain.com` with your actual domain name and update the paths to your SSL certificate and private key.

#### 2. Enable the Configuration

Create a symbolic link to enable the configuration:

```sh
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/ssl_termination /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
```

#### 3. Test the Configuration

Before restarting Nginx, test the configuration to ensure there are no syntax errors:

```sh
sudo nginx -t
```

#### 4. Restart Nginx

Restart Nginx to apply the changes:

```sh
sudo systemctl restart nginx
```

### Step 4: Configure Backend Servers

Ensure your backend servers are configured to accept plain HTTP traffic on the ports specified in the `upstream` block.

### Example Backend Server Configuration

Here's an example of how you might configure a simple Flask application to listen on port 8080:

```python
